Subject: register VM: free the nip_catch pop table on the success path

What this does
  js_create_function() has two exits. The fail path calls js_free_function_def(),
  which frees fd->regvm_nip_pop. The success path frees only fd->regvm_code
  (once it has been copied into the bytecode object) and then drops fd with
  free_objlit_sites() + js_free(), so fd->regvm_nip_pop — a uint16_t[bc_len]
  table that compute_stack_size() allocates whenever an OP_nip_catch pops a
  count other than 2 — was leaked.

  The table is read only while compiling (js_regvm_scan and the lowering), so
  freeing it on the success path is safe.

Why we need it
  Any register-compiled function whose body yields through a for-of/finally
  frame (e.g. `o.f = yield` inside a finally) leaked one block. It is reported
  only in debug builds because the leak report is compiled under ENABLE_DUMPS,
  which NDEBUG disables; the allocation itself happened in every build.

What it adds
  quickjs.c
      js_free(ctx, fd->regvm_nip_pop) before free_objlit_sites() in the
      success path of js_create_function().

  A no-op unless QJS_REGVM.

diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
index aab9dc3..eb346f5 100644
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -66794,6 +66794,12 @@ static JSValue js_create_function(JSContext *ctx, JSFunctionDef *fd)
         list_del(&fd->link);
     }
 
+#if QJS_REGVM
+    /* fd->regvm_code was already freed above once it was copied into b; the
+       nip_catch pop table has no other owner and must not ride out with fd. */
+    js_free(ctx, fd->regvm_nip_pop);
+    fd->regvm_nip_pop = NULL;
+#endif
     free_objlit_sites(ctx, fd);
     js_free(ctx, fd);
     return JS_MKPTR(JS_TAG_FUNCTION_BYTECODE, b);
